  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nIt's a sunny and dry day as a surface ridge remains anchored over\nthe Mid MS Valley. As an upper shortwave over the Red River \nValley, trailing the broader upper trough over the East Coast, \nshifts southeastward toward the northern Gulf tomorrow into \nMonday, a surface low will track through SE Texas into the Gulf. \nHigh clouds will increase ahead of this system tonight. This will \ninhibit stronger radiational cooling from taking place during the\novernight, and for this reason, a few Delta counties were trimmed\nfrom the Freeze Warning for tonight. Nevertheless, temps are \nstill expected to dip to near or below freezing over roughly the \nnortheast quarter of the forecast area by tomorrow morning, and a\nFreeze Warning remains in tact for these areas. As this system \ntracks south of our area, overrunning moisture will bring \nscattered sprinkles or possibly rain showers into the area later\nin the day Sunday. However, given the very dry low level airmass,\nmeasurable rainfall will be difficult to come by. A few central \nLA and south MS locations could pick up negligible amounts. The \nsurface ridge will build back in through the day Monday as this \nsystem shifts south of our area.\n\nNear freezing temps are possible again Monday and Tuesday \nmornings over northeast MS, but as we move beyond the growing \nseason and with those areas already having received a freeze this\nweekend, no additional freeze products are planned. Otherwise, \ntemps will gradually moderate through the remainder of the week. \nIn addition, dry conditions are expected as surface ridging\nremains posted over the eastern CONUS. A pair of late week \nshortwaves will pass well north of the area, teasing potential for\nrain by the weekend, but the probability of these resulting in \nlegitimate rainfall in our area is low at this time. /DL/",
